      "side": 1,
      "message": "Nit : while this will work, it tends to be less error-prone to write this kind of code declaratively rather than iteratively. In particular, this starts playing a big role if at any point later in time we use this member somewhere else.\n\nWhat you\u0027d do is have a final long that corresponds to the base that you\u0027d initialize with something like\n\n    base \u003d current time % frequency\n\nThen you always set the next alarm to\n\n    (current time + frequency) % frequency + base\n\nThe advantage of doing something like this is that it only ever uses constants and as such it is extremely robust against using the times somewhere else.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 335,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 335,
        "endChar": 34
      },
      "revId": "d65bec48724eb5274f3445776c0682c6c35f65c0",
